[38;5;252m[1m### Bug 1: [38;5;10mrng.rs[0m — AES-256-ECB output buffer too small[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The [38;5;10maes256_ecb[0m function passed the caller's 16-byte [38;5;10mbuffer[0m slice directly to OpenSSL's [38;5;10mCrypter::update()[0m. The Rust OpenSSL wrapper requires the output buffer to be at least [38;5;10mblock_size + input_length[0m = 32 bytes, causing a panic. The C code uses [38;5;10mEVP_EncryptUpdate[0m which writes exactly 16 bytes in-place without this constraint.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mFix:[22m Use a 32-byte temporary buffer for the OpenSSL call, then copy the first 16 bytes to the output.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[38;5;252m[1m### Bug 2: [38;5;10mblake256.rs[0m — [38;5;10mblake256_update[0m buffer offset not reset after compress[0m[0m
[0m[0m
